 ### Data Structures
-*   **Scene Parameters ($\theta$):** Stored as a flat vector or structured object containing differentiable attributes for each material and light source. For spatially-varying parameters (e.g., texture maps), gradients are stored as fields aligned with the surface geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scene Parameters ($\theta$):** Stored as a structured object containing differentiable attributes for each material and light source [S1, unofficial]. This work focuses on uniform material parameters; spatially-varying saliency fields are left to future work [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Rendered Image ($I$):** A tensor of shape $H \times W \times C$ (height, width, channels) representing the generalized sensor response [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Material Identity Map:** A per-pixel map generated by tracing primary rays from the camera, identifying the hit scene object and its associated material parameters [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Numbered Steps
+1. Render an image $I$ through a differentiable pipeline using $I = R(\theta)$ [S1, unofficial].
+2. Evaluate a scalar metric $M$ on the rendered image [S1, unofficial].
+3. Backpropagate through both the metric and the renderer to obtain per-parameter gradients $g = \partial M / \partial \theta$ [S1, unofficial].
+4. Map the obtained per-parameter gradients back to image space for visualization by tracing primary rays from the camera, identifying hit scene objects, and coloring each pixel with the saliency value of the visible material [S1, unofficial].
 
